From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 14:40:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Keep a character's costume consistent across every scene by
 default
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Costume uniformity was opt-out rather than opt-in. The Video Scene Director's
rule 3 read "Default to <locked outfit> unless the story or the wardrobe
instructions call for a change at that moment", which actively invited the model
to redress the character on story grounds alone — so a user who never asked for
a costume change could still get one, and the same character would walk through
their own video in three different outfits.

Costume changes now require the user to have written wardrobe notes, enforced in
two layers. The prompt layer states the costume is fixed for the whole video and
drops the story-driven escape hatch entirely when there are no wardrobe notes;
when there are, changes are permitted only where those notes call for one. The
parser layer is the actual guarantee: with no wardrobe notes the locked outfit
is pinned onto every scene regardless of what the model replied, so a director
that ignores its instructions still cannot change the character's clothes.
Whitespace-only notes count as no notes. Character identity itself was already
sound and is untouched — every keyframe is an image edit of the outfit's
reference photo, so the face comes from a real picture rather than a re-roll.

AI b-roll had the matching problem one level up: every scene's image prompt was
planned independently, so palette, light and grade drifted scene to scene and a
finished video read as a stock-photo collage. The Art Director now also returns
a single style clause that is appended to every scene prompt, giving one look
across the video while leaving each scene's subject free. Deliberately not the
image-anchoring trick that character mode uses — b-roll scenes are meant to show
different things, and anchoring scene two on scene one's picture would drag its
subject along with its style. The clause is length-bounded and the existing
fail-soft behaviour is preserved exactly: no usable style leaves the prompts
byte-identical to before, and a failed planning call still falls back to
narration text.

